  "Many people blame a lack of willpower when getting up in the morning feels like a battle or when sleep refuses to come at night. Researchers who study the body’s internal clock, however, suggest that the cause may often lie elsewhere. {u1|What they have increasingly come to believe is that the less natural light people receive early in the day, the later their bodies prepare for sleep, and the harder it becomes for them to wake up refreshed.} Because modern life keeps so many of us indoors during the morning hours, this finding may help to explain why tiredness has become such a common complaint.",
  "Almost every cell in the human body follows a rhythm of roughly twenty-four hours, known as the circadian* rhythm. This rhythm controls when we feel alert, when our body temperature rises and falls, and when the brain releases melatonin*, a hormone that signals that it is time to rest. Left to itself, however, the internal clock of most people runs slightly longer than twenty-four hours. It therefore needs to be reset every day, and the most powerful signal for doing so is light, especially bright light in the morning. Light entering the eyes tells the brain that the day has begun, so that melatonin is released at an appropriate time that evening.",
  "{u2|In one study described in a health report, about two hundred office workers wore small devices on their wrists for two weeks that recorded both the amount of light they were exposed to and the times at which they fell asleep and woke up.} The participants also answered short questionnaires every morning about how rested they felt. Those who spent at least thirty minutes outdoors before ten o’clock fell asleep, on average, about forty minutes earlier than those who stayed indoors all morning. They also said they felt more alert through the day and were less likely to rely on coffee in the afternoon. Interestingly, the effect appeared even on cloudy days, because daylight outdoors is still many times brighter than the light in a typical office.",
  "Why should morning light make such a difference? Researchers have suggested two main explanations. The first is that bright light in the morning moves the internal clock earlier, so that the brain begins releasing melatonin sooner in the evening and the person naturally feels sleepy at a reasonable hour. The second concerns what happens in the evening. People who receive little light in the morning often spend their evenings in front of bright screens, and this light can delay the release of melatonin even further. A person whose clock has not been reset in the morning is therefore doubly affected: the signal to wake up is weak, and the signal to sleep comes late.",
